## 3. The Gap

Overlaying the literature above reveals a precise, previously unoccupied space.

| | Studies... | Using... | Finds... |
|---|---|---|---|
| Calibration literature (Kadavath, Tian, Leng, Chhikara) | Whether stated/implied confidence matches accuracy | Token probabilities, verbalized confidence scores | RLHF systematically degrades calibration |
| Faithfulness literature (Turpin, Lanham) | Whether CoT reflects the true causal process | Input perturbation, activation patching | CoT can be causally disconnected from the answer, especially under adversarial input bias |
| Faithful uncertainty literature (Yona, MetaFaith) | Whether the **full response** linguistically communicates intrinsic uncertainty | Example-level faithfulness metrics comparing intrinsic confidence to linguistic decisiveness | LLMs largely fail to hedge appropriately across the response as a whole |
| UQ methods (Semantic Entropy, EVSE, Pairwise Similarity) | How to estimate internal answer uncertainty accurately | Multi-sample clustering, evidence theory | Provide increasingly precise measures of $H(\text{answer} \mid q)$ |
| Agentic explainability surveys | Whether post-hoc/interactive methods can explain agent behavior | Taxonomy and framework proposals | Post-hoc methods are widely used but explicitly flagged as inadequate; faithfulness of agent self-reflection is untested |

**What has not been asked, by any of the above:** given a model that has *already committed to a specific answer*, and is *then separately asked to explain that answer* — a structurally post-hoc generation step, architecturally decoupled from whatever internal state produced the original answer — does the resulting explanation (a) communicate the degree of internal uncertainty that existed at the moment of commitment, and (b) distinguish, in its rhetorical stance, between justifying a true claim and justifying a false one?

This is a narrower and more mechanistic question than "is the full response faithfully hedged" (Yona/MetaFaith), and a different question than "is the reasoning causally connected to the answer under adversarial bias" (Turpin/Lanham). It isolates the explanation-generation step itself, treats it as architecturally separate from answer generation (a fresh forward pass conditioned only on the externalized question-and-answer text, with no access to the hidden states that produced the answer), and asks two very specific, falsifiable things: does it carry forward quantitative uncertainty, and does it perform truth-blind justification.

